  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/f/f9/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_115.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Reed summons Johnny to the Baxter Building to give him a solo assignment, as he's busy with trying to find a cure for Alicia's blindness, and Ben and Sue are tied up with a report on the FF's encounter with the Molecule Man.&nbsp; This leaves the Human Torch free to go after the Sandman, who's just escaped from prison.\n\nAfter the two of them review the Sandman's origin, abilities, and his initial bout with Spider-Man (and how easy it was for the Sandman to escape from prison by transforming his body into sand and slipping through the bars of his cell), Johnny takes issue with the fact that Reed doesn't want him to go after the Sandman, but instead find Spider-Man and allow him to bring the Sandman in!&nbsp; <i>Johnny is incensed by the very <b>idea</b>!</i>\n\nThe Human Torch is ultimately unable to <i>find</i> Spider-Man, but he <i>does</i> spot the Sandman on the George Washington Bridge, surrounded by police cars.&nbsp; Sandman has no time for him -- he only wants <i>Spider-Man</i> -- and slips away, hiding <i>under</i> the bridge.&nbsp; Johnny is unable to find him.\n\nRealizing Sandman wants only Spider-Man, Johnny dresses up in the wall-crawler's costume to try and lure him out, calling out to him from atop the Empire State Building.&nbsp; Sandman sees him on a television broadcast and immediately makes his way toward the skyscraper, sneaking up behind Johnny and knocking him off the building.&nbsp; But the Human Torch has no problem flaming on and streaking back up to face the Sandman.\n\nThough surprised to find that he'd just attacked the Human Torch instead of his intended victim, Sandman shows no alarm and jumps down an elevator shaft to wait, hoping that Johnny's flame will die down soon, which will give him an advantage, even <i>more so</i> because \"sand doesn't burn!\"\n\nEventually, both of them find themselves beneath the building's sprinklers, which affect <i>both</i> opponents, neither able to fully use their powers.&nbsp; But though the Sandman indicates, \"I'm almost <i>twice </i>your size.\"&nbsp; But Johnny has been taught \"rough and tumble\" by the Thing and the use of \"judo and karate\" by Mr. Fantastic.\n\nForced into hand-to-hand combat, Johnny eventually outmaneuvers the Sandman, knocking him out, and turning him over to the police, just as the <i>real</i> Spider-Man arrives on the scene in time to see the Sandman taken away, thinking that \"sooner or later, this town will be <i>too small</i> for the Torch and me!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "A routine Torch adventure with very few clever touches to lift it above serviceable.\nYou move through it easily enough, even as you recognize it won't linger long.",
  "subtitle": "Burning Someone Else's Bridge",
  "review_text": "I got a kick out of the whole notion on the splash page that [SPAN: severity=info; text=Sandman appears by \"special arrangement with the <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> magazine!\"], as if Stan and company needed permission to use the character in another Marvel comic, <i>heh</i>.\n\nIt was good to see the Sandman back in action in his second appearance (his first was a mere three months earlier, in [LINK:1963:ASM_4|ASM #4|2026-10-26]), this time against a <i>different</i> opponent.&nbsp; But... was he really Spider-Man's [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=\"fatal foe,\"] as the cover indicates?\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=Who exactly did he previously kill, I ask you?]\n\n[ASSET: left | JuniorMember | width=15%] Reed continues to order Johnny around far too often for my taste.&nbsp; He and the other FF members keep calling him a teenager and \"kid,\" and it seems like they go out of their way to remind him he's the junior member of the team.\n[BR]\n[BR] Reed even thinks (on page 4) that \"like <i>any</i> normal teenager, he <i>needs</i> a dressing down once in a while!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=Geez!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | TooDumb | width=25%] Sandman seemed unusually fixated on fighting Spider-Man, even saying that he didn't have time to fight \"kids\" like the Human Torch (I guess not realizing that Spider-Man is one <i>himself</i>).\n[BR]\n[BR] And it seemed odd that he later thought the Torch was too dumb to figure out that he was trying to manipulate him into using up his flame, when it's really Flint Marko, [SPAN: severity=info; text=who is no Einstein.]\n\n[ASSET: center | UnderTheSprinklers | width=67%]\nThe fact that neither opponent was fully able to use their powers under the sprinklers was clever enough, but page 12 suggests that Johnny's strength more than <i>doubles</i> simply because his body has \"excess heat,\" [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=a claim I found more than a little unconvincing.]\n[ASSET: center | Unconvincing | width=67%]\n\nHe'd already indicated that he'd received training in hand-to-hand combat by both the Thing and Mr. Fantastic.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=That should have sufficed], making the extra‑strength comment totally unnecessary.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIt was interesting to see Spider-Man drawn (for the first <i>time</i>, I believe) by someone <i>other</i> than Steve Ditko or Jack Kirby (even though most of the drawings were of Johnny, <i>dressed</i> as Spider-Man -- at least until the final page when the <i>real </i>one showed up).&nbsp; Still, Dick Ayers did a decent rendition of the wall-crawler that I thought was perfectly serviceable.\n\nSpider-Man's consternation that the Human Torch went after the Sandman (who he <i>clearly</i> seemed to think was <i>his</i> responsibility) was laughable, and the final panel that hints that the town [SPAN: severity=warning; text=might not be big enough for both of them] elicited a brief chuckle.\n[ASSET: center | TownAintBigEnough | width=67%]\n\nI also found the fact that the Sandman walked away meekly with police officers on the final page without even attempting the obvious escape of turning into sand particles again, [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=utterly ridiculous.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text= You're not even going to make a break for it, Sandy?&nbsp; <b>Seriously?</b>]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nWhile it was nice to see the Sandman back in action again, the mechanics of the story were still largely mundane, and it felt at times like a plotter and artist simply going through the motions.\n\n<i>True</i>... its primary draw was the fact that the Human Torch was going against a foe that had previously menaced someone <i>else</i> (and admittedly, the Spider-Man cameo at the end was [SPAN: severity=success; text=nice to see]), but the issue's final grade was more a result of seeing Sandman and Spider-Man than of any inherent quality in the conflict itself.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=Yet I feel I should give a little love to the cover] (which I believe earns the book a slight bit of extra credit here) -- a bold, dramatic Torch‑vs‑Sandman splash that promises more than the interior delivers, but still adds to the overall enjoyment.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Maybe it helped that I actually owned a copy and got to see it up close -- it was a cover I genuinely enjoyed looking at.)]\n\nI also liked the fact that the Human Torch found a way to prevail without the use of his flame, even while pointing out above how his enhanced strength [SPAN: severity=warning; text=stretched credibility a bit.]\n\nThis was largely a run-of-the-mill plot that was enjoyable in the moment, but not likely to linger long in the minds of many readers.",
  "side_note": [
    "[ASSET: left | ExpensiveTV | width=20%] On page 7, we see the Sandman in a department store looking at televisions.&nbsp; One is on sale for $199.95, which I found an absolutely <i>astonishing</i> amount for 1963!&nbsp; This was particularly eye-opening, as my wife -- just <i>yesterday</i> in fact -- purchased a 65 inch television for around $380, and this is <i>60 years later</i> (as of this writing in 2023)!\n[BR]\n[BR] The one Sandman would have been looking at would certainly have been a black-and-white TV, complete with <i>tubes</i>!&nbsp; Color televisions would have cost no less than $400 at the time, so this must truly have been a top-of-the-line black-and-white model for its day.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=Wow!]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Yes</i>, I could have turned this into a Vintage Moment, but this issue already provided a <b><i>better</i></b><i> </i>one, as you'll see in this month's summary.)]",
    "Despite the fact that [LINK:1963:FF_21|FF #21|2027-01-24] (the issue <i>after</i> the team's encounter with the Molecule Man) was published on the same day as this issue, it <i>appears</i> that the events of this issue may take place between [LINK:1963:FF_20|FF #20|2026-12-25] and [LINK:1963:FF_21|FF #21|2027-01-24] (since otherwise, you'd expect that Ben and Sue would be writing about their encounter with the <i>Hate Monger</i> instead of the <i>Molecule Man</i>).\n\nBut as I laid out in my [PAGE: reading-order], unless the text of the issue <i>explicitly states</i> that its events precede another's (given the books are otherwise tied in terms of publication date), [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=I don't assume it], so my alphabetical order rule still applies, meaning that I present [LINK:1963:FF_21|FF #21|2027-01-24] before <i>Strange Tales</i> in the reading order."
  ],
  "ownership": "I got a roughly VG/FN copy as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid almost $42.&nbsp; It sold on eBay in July 2020 for $175, netting me almost $110.",
  "decoder_ring": "In the pages of <i>Strange Tales</i>, Johnny has now faced all three regular members of what will eventually make up the Frightful Four:&nbsp; the Wizard, Paste-Pot Pete (later the Trapster), and now Sandman.&nbsp; The stage is being set for the Frightful Four, but of course, that's still over a year away...",
  "highlight": "Human Torch versus the Sandman -- a fiery detour into Spider‑Man's corner of the universe."
}